# **Four things at once: light through the crest, the bed through the column,
# foam, and spray — from a camera above the water.**
#
#     loom render assets/test/lucent.loom --sim 300 --size 1920x1080 -o /tmp/lucent.png
#     loom-scene lucent --play
#
# Every other sea in this repository shows one of those. `ocean_tropical` is the
# colour, `whitecaps` the foam, `spindrift` the spray, `shore` the shallow band.
# This one has to hold all four in one frame, and they do not want the same
# scene.
#
# # The tension, and how it is settled
#
# **Seeing the bed wants shallow, clear, calm water. Foam and spray want a
# steep, energetic sea. Backlighting wants a low sun; seeing *into* water wants
# a high one.** Three fights, and the settlement is different in each.
#
# **Steep and shallow is not a contradiction here, and the reason is `k`.**
# `fold` — the quantity the whitecaps, the spray and the breaking test all key
# on — is `Σ steepness·k·A·sin φ` (`loom_water/src/lib.rs:388`), so it is
# governed by `k·A`: **wave height over wavelength, not wave height.** Short
# waves have large `k`, so a low sea of short chop reaches the same fold as a
# tall sea of long swell. The three waves below:
#
#     lambda 13.0  A 0.62  steepness 0.80   k 0.4833   s*k*A 0.2397
#     lambda  7.5  A 0.34  steepness 0.82   k 0.8378   s*k*A 0.2336
#     lambda  5.0  A 0.20  steepness 0.82   k 1.2566   s*k*A 0.2061
#                                       fold ceiling =  0.6794
#
# — twice `WATER_FOAM_BREAK` and `SPRAY_BREAK`, both 0.33, while the amplitudes
# sum to a crest of only **1.16 m**. That is what lets a breaking sea sit over
# three metres of water.
#
# **The validator's cap is a different quantity and it is easy to confuse them.**
# It refuses `steepness·k·A > 1/N` *per wave* — 0.333 here — which is the
# self-intersection bound. Each of the three sits at about 0.7 of it.
#
# **Low sun and a visible bed is settled by looking down rather than by raising
# the sun.** The camera is 4.5 m up and pitched 16° down, so the near half of
# the frame is water seen at 25-45° from the normal — inside Fresnel's
# see-into-it band — while the far half grazes and reads as sky and crest. The
# sun sits at **6.9° of elevation, 19° off the camera's bearing**, which puts it
# behind the water the lens is pointed at.
#
# **6.9° is not a mood, it is the condition the subsurface term needs.** After
# the wrap lobe came out (see `scene.slang`'s long comment at the term), the
# backlit-crest path is gated on `saturate(-dot(normal, sunDirection()))` —
# Lambert's cosine on the face the light enters, which for a thin sheet is the
# one *behind* the face you can see. That is non-zero only where the visible
# face is tilted further from the vertical than the sun is above the horizon.
# `ocean_tropical` is the counter-example and it is measured: over a 36-point
# grid at its own render instant its steepest *resolved* face is **9.6°** and its
# sun is 38.3° up, so almost nothing on it can be backlit at all and its
# subsurface term is correctly nearly silent. The same grid on this sea reads
# **39.4°** against a 6.9° sun. (Both are the Gerstner normal; the shading normal
# adds the Cox–Munk capillary slope on top, which is what softens the cosine at
# the terminator instead of a wrap constant.)
#
# # The four things, each a number
#
# Measured at `--sim 300`, 1280x720, at this camera. `LOOM_ABLATE` switches an
# effect off; the fraction is `loom compare` at **zero** tolerance against the
# same frame with it on.
#
#     the bed through the water   71.66% of the frame   mean 13.62   worst 145
#     subsurface glow             14.24%                mean  0.67   worst  95
#     foam                        25.94%                mean  3.41   worst 134
#     spray                        0.56%                mean  0.17   worst 186
#
# Foam's mean and spray's area both roughly doubled at the foam-density pass
# (`foamAge` was calling 0.33-second-old foam ten seconds old, and the crest
# crown was seven identical beads).
#
# **Foam then fell 55.48% -> 25.94% and that is the fix, not a loss.** The
# vertex foam trail was seeded from `WATER_FOAM_WET` — it remembered a crest
# that got *wet* rather than one that *broke* — so `in.foamHist` was above 0.05
# on 100% of this frame's water at a mean of 0.344, which put the erosion
# noise's near-binary threshold in its most discriminating band over the entire
# sea and painted half of it in 6x-stretched lace. From 1.4 m over the water it
# read as torn ribbons and nested contour rings. The trail now remembers
# `WATER_FOAM_BREAK`/`_BROKEN`, the pair the shader already paints white with,
# and drains on `WATER_FOAM_AGE_SECONDS`. The glow rises with it because foam
# now covers less of the water the glow was lighting.
#
# **Water is 77.40% of this frame** (measured the same way, against a render with
# the `Water` node deleted), so the bed is doing visible work over **92.6% of the
# water**. Spray's fraction is small and its worst channel is the largest in the
# table, which is what a scatter of bright sub-metre droplets looks like to a
# pixel diff: **3,339 droplets are in the air at this instant**, and the same
# scene with `spray = 0.0` renders `particles: 0`.
#
# The bed's number and the spray's are against edited copies of this scene rather
# than against ablations, because neither is a switchable effect: `behind * T` is
# the refraction path itself, and spray is a scene-authored population.
#
# **Off the screen and onto the sea**, from
# `loom_cli`'s `lucent_breaks_and_throws` — a 251×251 grid over 160 m at the same
# instant:
#
#     mu_max mean 0.1278   past 0.22 (foam onset) 26.24%   past 0.33 (breaking) 11.15%
#     foam painted over 14.10% of the surface      3,339 droplets in the air
#
# **14.1% painted is a stylised sea and it should be read as one.** `scene.slang`
# records `ocean`'s measured coverage as 4–7% and Monahan's whitecap relation at
# that scene's 14 m/s as 3.2%; this sea is at 11 m/s and about three times
# whiter than either. It is authored that way because whitecaps and spray have to
# be legible in a *still*, and because the same `steepness` that makes them is
# what makes the crests thin enough for the subsurface term to light. If the
# breaking limiter ever lands, this is the first scene it should be re-argued
# against.
#
# # What this scene is NOT showing, and why
#
# **It has no surf, and it must not be read as having any.** `SEA-REBUILD.md`
# §4.1's depth-limited breaking limiter and §5's curl sheet are both unbuilt. So:
#
# - **Nothing here enforces the depth limit; the scene is authored to respect
#   it.** Munk's breaker index puts a wave's height at breaking at about
#   `0.78 · h`. The deterministic maximum here — all three waves in phase — is
#   `H = 2 × 1.16 = 2.32 m` over `h = 3.2 m`, so `H/h = 0.725`, inside 0.78. The
#   `Hs` the engine reports is 2.078 m (`4σ` of the three-wave sum), `Hs/h =
#   0.65`. Those numbers are checked, not hoped: if this wave set is ever made
#   taller, it goes past the index and the sea becomes a thing that cannot
#   physically stand there.
# - **`attenuation_depth` is left at its default zero, so nothing shoals.** A
#   13 m swell over 3.2 m of water is `kh = 1.55` — intermediate water, where a
#   real wave would already have slowed, shortened, steepened and broken. What
#   is drawn is a deep-water Gerstner sea with a bed painted underneath it.
#   Turning shoaling on would flatten exactly the crests this scene exists to
#   backlight, and `shoal()` has no breaking limiter behind it to put the energy
#   anywhere else.
# - **A crest here goes white and throws droplets; it never plunges.** There is
#   no curl, no lip, no barrel and no broken-water sheet after the break.
#
# # The water
#
# **Jerlov II**, the middle open-ocean row of `WaterOptics`'s table: `K_d` =
# 0.3712 / 0.0760 / 0.0383 and `b_b` = 0.00528 / 0.00727 / 0.01091, `G/B` 0.394.
# Jerlov III (`ocean_tropical`'s row) was rendered beside it and is more
# turquoise but hazes the bed out; Jerlov IB shows the bed most sharply and goes
# navy in the troughs. II is where both halves are legible at once, which is the
# whole request.
#
# **3.2 m of water, and the shelf is 200 m square.** Its far edge is a
# discontinuity — the same trap `shore.loom` and `ocean_tropical.loom` name — and
# `fog_density = 0.008` is what hides it. The bed is bright carbonate sand,
# nearly neutral, so the colour in the frame is the water's.
#
# # The camera, and the two alternatives
#
# Swept at 1280x720 and kept in `.superpowers/sdd/sea-showcase/`:
#
#     waterline   eye 2.2 m, pitch  -8°   one wave face fills the frame; most
#                                         drama, least bed
#     shelf       eye 4.5 m, pitch -16°   this one — bed and crests both legible
#     overlook    eye 8.0 m, pitch -26°   the whole wave field and the most bed,
#                                         but the sea reads as corrugation
#
# Sun elevation was swept at 6.9 / 10.1 / 19.9°: at 20° the glow washes flat over
# the whole sea and the wave structure goes with it, which is the failure mode
# the subsurface term's own comment records.
#
# **No boat.** `ocean_fft_boat.loom` is the scene with scale in it; a hull here
# covers the water that is the subject. The `Marker` is `spindrift.loom`'s
# device instead — one 3.6 m pile, 20 cm square, off to the left — because a
# still of open water has no scale in it and a droplet could be any size at all.

[scene]
format = 1
id = "6b2e4f19-30ad-4c7e-b158-9e0d7a5c2f84"

[[node]]
name = "Sea"

  # A low sun, 19° off the lens's bearing rather than straight down it, so the
  # glitter path is beside the subject instead of on top of it. The sky is
  # deliberately not a saturated blue: whatever the sea reflects should be near
  # neutral, or a blue sky makes a blue sea for a reason that has nothing to do
  # with the water.
  [node.components.Environment]
  sun_direction = [0.33, 0.12, -0.936]
  sun_strength = 1.8
  sun_color = [1.0, 0.94, 0.84]
  ambient = 0.34
  sky_zenith = [0.10, 0.26, 0.56]
  sky_horizon = [0.58, 0.66, 0.74]
  fog_density = 0.008
  fog_falloff = 0.05

  # 11 m/s. The wind does two things here that the wave set does not: it drives
  # the Cox–Munk capillary slope the shading normal carries (`WATER_DETAIL_*`),
  # which is what softens the backlit cosine at the terminator, and it sets the
  # foam's own wander.
  [node.components.Wind]
  direction_degrees = 190.0
  speed = 11.0
  gustiness = 1.0
  turbulence = 0.9

# **The shelf: bright sand, 3.2 m down, 200 m square.** One box op at a
# one-metre voxel — a flat bed needs nothing finer and the cost is cubic in the
# resolution. The volume's near corner is its node, so this spans x −90..90 and
# z −130..50; the camera stands at z = 16 and looks toward −Z, so there are
# 146 m of shelf in front of it.
[[node]]
name = "Shelf"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [-90.0, -24.0, -130.0] }

  [node.components.VoxelVolume]
  voxel_size = 1.0
  chunks = [6, 1, 6]

  [[node.components.VoxelVolume.ops]]
  kind = "box"
  center = [96.0, 10.8, 96.0]
  half_extents = [98.0, 10.0, 98.0]
  mode = "union"

  # Carbonate sand: bright and nearly neutral. A yellow bed would put its own
  # hue in the frame, and the hue here is supposed to be the water's.
  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.84, 0.80, 0.70]
  roughness = 0.92

[[node]]
name = "Water"
parent = "Sea"

  [node.components.WaterBody]
  kind = "ocean"
  surface_height = 0.0
  density = 1025.0
  drag = 2.0
  # How many crests throw, not how hard: a droplet's arc is what the crest that
  # threw it can do. `spindrift.loom`'s value, on a sea with a comparable fold
  # ceiling. Zero is the default and is every sea that predates W5.
  spray = 8.0

    # Jerlov II — a published row, not a colour somebody liked. See the header
    # for why this row and not the two either side of it, and `WaterOptics` for
    # the table and its sources.
    [node.components.WaterBody.optics]
    attenuation = [0.3712, 0.0760, 0.0383]
    backscatter = [0.00528, 0.00727, 0.01091]

  # **Three waves with no common factor between their wavelengths**, each
  # carrying about a third of the fold budget, so the sum passes 0.33 where two
  # of them crest together — a few percent of the surface at any instant, which
  # is what a breaking sea looks like. One wave at the same total steepness
  # breaks in a corrugated line right across the scene.
  #
  # Nothing shorter than 5 m: the Nyquist fade deletes waves under about four
  # metres at range, and a wave that is there in the near field and gone in the
  # mid field is worse than one that was never authored.
  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 13.0
  amplitude = 0.62
  steepness = 0.80
  direction = [1.0, 0.2]
  speed_scale = 1.0

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 7.5
  amplitude = 0.34
  steepness = 0.82
  direction = [0.85, -0.5]
  speed_scale = 1.0

  [[node.components.WaterBody.waves.waves]]
  wavelength = 5.0
  amplitude = 0.20
  steepness = 0.82
  direction = [1.0, 0.62]
  speed_scale = 1.0

# What a droplet off this water looks like. A `ParticleEmitter` under the
# `WaterBody` is the water's splash and never plays where it sits — spray
# borrows its colour and size rather than inventing a second answer to "what
# does this water throw".
[[node]]
name = "Spray"
parent = "Sea/Water"

  [node.components.ParticleEmitter]
  rate = 0.0
  burst = 70
  lifetime = 0.8
  lifetime_jitter = 0.4
  speed = 4.5
  spread_degrees = 55.0
  radius = 0.4
  gravity = -9.0
  drag = 0.5
  turbulence = 0.6
  turbulence_scale = 0.8
  # **A sprite here is a fistful of torn water, not one drop.** 15 cm was the
  # literal reading and it is under two pixels at the range this spray lives at
  # — 15 to 34 m, the far end of `SPRAY_RANGE` — so the crowns read as a scatter
  # of isolated beads and vanished entirely at the 800 px the GIF is judged at.
  # Measured on this frame downscaled to 800: 0.15 -> 0.22 takes legible spray
  # from 0.12% of the frame to 0.37%. 0.30 was rendered too and goes back to
  # reading as blobs — the marker beside it is 20 cm square, and past about one
  # marker width a droplet stops being water in the air.
  size = [0.22, 0.06]
  color_start = [0.95, 0.98, 1.0]
  color_end = [0.70, 0.80, 0.88]
  alpha = [0.9, 0.0]
  seed = 61

# The scale reference. 3.6 m of 20 cm pile, half of it under water — so it also
# carries the waterline band the gap-to-solid foam draws, which nothing else in
# this frame shows.
[[node]]
name = "Marker"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [-4.2, 0.7, -7.0], scale = [0.20, 3.6, 0.20] }

  [node.components.MeshRenderer]
  mesh = { asset = "box" }

  [node.components.Material]
  albedo = [0.16, 0.14, 0.12]
  roughness = 0.85

# See the header's sweep. 4.5 m up and 16° down is the one place both the bed
# and the backlit crests are legible in the same frame.
[[node]]
name = "Camera"
parent = "Sea"
transform = { pos = [0.0, 4.5, 16.0], rot_euler = [-16.0, 0.0, 0.0] }

  [node.components.Camera]
  fov_y_degrees = 55.0
